17 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417823 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186417820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417823 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b1e2a497-e042-518c-bac4-15b77f002a69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417823 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186417816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428459 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428459 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1930->4386. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428459 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186049070 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186435505 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435505 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435505 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18d294d7-e90d-595c-8fa7-6170debebbf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435506 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435506 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435506 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "519adb21-ac16-5108-9bc4-9df2a4611d46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435507 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435507 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435507 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5629f73f-1165-5287-a036-29dde44376aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435508 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435508 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435508 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a0ee6fb8-0275-543e-9a03-ba5f9eb26748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435509 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435509 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435509 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "af171406-b374-5cc4-8490-0e1a9e1865ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435510 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435510 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435510 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435510 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435510 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435510 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435510 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435510 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435510 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e0d75057-311d-52aa-8f97-e8208efd3230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435511 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435511 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435511 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435511 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7c/7cb1268b1007611ba22364c23439e329f33d806a585bffb497b40a0c1e5f316e.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-2,1)\\cup (1,3)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435511 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4212543c-3dc3-579d-b570-8f3b6f775fcc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435511 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb77fe9590ad2dfd4ff0259da48765eb3539e58590f585f4530d121aea1553ee.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ Notice that $B$ does not belong to $M$ and $B$ belongs to any open neighborhood of $B.$\n\n![](images/1d/1dc73455fa8f3a82dded795ccb1e375fe32765f2279a654f1694e8b4cb5aed7f.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/4f/4f9ed1e3fa6b3232cb8f37d756e11034563722080e769b351d0e4ebeb0c4ecc3.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
